## Mandatory Test Style

**`fluent-tests` is the mandatory test style for this repo.** Every test — new, refactored, or fixed — must comply with the `fluent-tests` skill at `.claude/skills/fluent-tests/`. Before writing or modifying any test, read:

- `.claude/skills/fluent-tests/references/generic-guidelines.md` (universal principles)
- The framework-specific reference for the test type (`rust/std-test.md`, `typescript/cypress-component.md`, etc.)

Required: Given/When/Then structure, intent-revealing test names, one behavior per test, named page-object/driver helpers (no raw selectors or wire-format handling in test bodies), meaningful fixture values, and in-memory backends instead of `cy.intercept` for Cypress component tests. Violations are treated as test bugs.

**Names describe the subject — file names included.** A test is named for what it tests, never for
the command, phase or changeset that produced it: `*_red.rs` is the same mistake as
`fn red_phase_rejects_…`, one level out. `unit`, `integration` and `e2e` **are** legitimate
markers — they state the scope and cost of the file and stay true for its life. `acceptance` is
not: it names no level, and the 348 files carrying it are mostly ordinary integration tests, so it
partitions nothing. Suffixes a runner discovers by (`.test.ts(x)`, `.cy.ts(x)`) always stay. See
[docs/dev/guides/testing.md](../../docs/dev/guides/testing.md) § Workflow Names in Test or File
Names.

